// Regression: [string]-array ELEMENT tracking is per-FUNCTION, not per-program.
//
// `register_str_array_var` keys on the variable NAME only, and is consulted to
// choose the element ACCESSOR (array_get_str vs array_get_int). Unlike its four
// siblings - reset_array_vars / reset_int_array_vars / reset_float_vars /
// reset_sb_vars - it had NO reset function at all, so the table accumulated
// every [string]-array local in the translation unit. A LATER local sharing a
// name with an earlier string array then read its elements through
// array_get_str, including when the array had been built with array_push_int:
//
//     fn a() { var ws = ["x","y"]; var xs = ws.map((s) => s.upper()); print(xs[0]) }
//     fn b() { var xs = [10, 20];                                     print(xs[0]) }
//
// b() printed an EMPTY LINE instead of 10, with exit status 0 - a silently wrong
// answer, not a compile error, so no gate that only checks exit codes could see
// it. Order-dependent: declare b() before a() and it is correct, which is why it
// survives casual review.
//
// This is the fifth table in that family to bite. It also blocks batching test
// arms into one program, since that is precisely what makes names collide.
//
// There are TWO per-function reset clusters: top-level functions reset in
// codegen_statement, module `pub fn`s in emit_function_with_prefix. Both are
// exercised here - deleting the module one alone left every top-level arm green.

// EXPECT: X
// EXPECT: 10
// EXPECT: 20
// EXPECT: B
// EXPECT: 2
// EXPECT: M
// EXPECT: 31

import strarr_leak_mod

// Registers `xs` as a [string] array (the .map result is typed [string]).
fn registers_str_array() -> string {
    var ws = ["x", "y"]
    var xs = ws.map((s) => s.upper())
    return xs[0]
}

// Same NAME, [int] elements. Without the reset these two arms printed raw HEAP
// ADDRESSES (measured: 4385252004 for the slot holding 10) - the int slot read
// back through array_get_str and then used as a number. Asserting the CORRECT
// value matters here: the wrong one is ASLR-dependent and differs every run.
fn same_name_int_array() -> int {
    var xs = [10, 20]
    return xs[0] + 0
}

fn same_name_int_array_second_slot() -> int {
    var xs = [10, 20]
    return xs[1] + 0
}

// The [string] branch must still work AFTER the [int] one, so this arm catches a
// fix that reset the table at the wrong moment and lost a live entry.
//
// It does NOT catch "drop the tracking altogether", and the distinction was
// measured rather than assumed: with registration disabled entirely, every arm
// here still passes, because a newer type-driven path already answers for these
// shapes. So nothing in the 320-program corpus exercises this registry in the
// POSITIVE direction — the registry may be redundant. That is a separate
// question from this leak, and it is filed rather than guessed at here.
fn str_array_again() -> string {
    var ws = ["a", "b"]
    var xs = ws.map((s) => s.upper())
    return xs[1]
}

fn str_array_len_again() -> int {
    var ws = ["a", "b"]
    var xs = ws.map((s) => s.upper())
    return xs.len()
}

print(registers_str_array())
print(same_name_int_array())
print(same_name_int_array_second_slot())
print(str_array_again())
print(str_array_len_again())
print(strarr_leak_mod.str_first())
print(strarr_leak_mod.int_second())
